# Babble examples (2026-11-16)
#
# One of four design reports grown from the babble seed. Its lens: better examples for spw.quest, along the planned improvements to the workbench's ocean, and what carries a stranger into factshift.center.
# Everything here is an open wonder for the owner to judge. Nothing is decided, planned or committed.
# Numbers were measured on 2026-11-16 with the workbench at cf41ca22. Timings are warm medians of 15 runs in node on one shared machine and move by about a sixth between runs; re-run before relying on one.
# The harness and every probe output sit beside this file in examples-scratch/.

#>babble_examples_2026_11_16
#:journal #!babble
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"found"{
 count: "spw.quest's shelf holds 26 files, not 24: Bench 7, Study 2, Board 2, Stage 3, Trial 4, Team 8."
 readers: "Every script was read by three readers: the workbench's current seed (cf41ca22), spw.quest's pinned parser (27fa1e3d, in the site consumer) and the reader factshift.center bundles (2026.11.07-pre.1, 3f53adf2). All 26 files, the five starters and the ten proposed scripts read complete on each, with 0 errors, 0 warnings and no prose fallback."
 quest_check_is_empty: "spw.quest's starters test counts errors from parse(source).diagnostics, a field its pinned parser's result does not carry; the result holds errors and warnings. Its parses-clean assertion therefore passes for any text. An unclosed brace gives 0 errors on all three readers and shows only as 1 warning, a prose fallback and complete false."
 canvas_cut: "factshift.center's statement splitter reads a # that follows a space, and is not followed by one of > : [ { ( @, as the start of a comment. On the canvas the pipeline file loses its #index step (10 of 11 steps arrive), and the algorithms file loses the tails of bfs, dfs and memo, so bfs, dfs, window, memo and the closing question fuse into one row with unclosed braces. 24 of 26 files lose every #!aim after their first tag."
 canvas_arrival: "The canvas does not hold a file's first line. It holds an arrival line picked by four rules, and 17 of 26 files fall through to the last, the first with parts of its own. The pipeline file arrives on its source line and not on its steps; 7 files arrive on a binding their frame names, 1 on an & line, 1 on a line that joins the others."
 held_is_heading: "The held line becomes the page's heading and title exactly as written, so a stranger's first read on factshift.center is one raw line of Spw. Across the 26 files that heading runs from 27 characters (review) to 362 (notebook)."
 no_pivot_on_arrival: "18 of 26 arrival lines are bindings (name = value), and pivotsOf returns no pivot for a binding as written: 17 of 26 files offer no pivot on the line the canvas holds. Read alone, the same values hold up to 24."
 anchor_pivots: "Every file's first line is notation: 25 open with a #>frame anchor and one with #[episode]{. pivotsOf offers 3 pivots on an anchor and each breaks it (#>lab_notebook becomes >lab_notebook#, .>lab_notebook or @>lab_notebook)."
 side_collapses: "The reader factshift.center bundles gives !(alarm) and (alarm)! the same slots, a word ! with a scene alarm. Today's canvas cannot show which side a mark sits on; the current seed's fragment reader keeps the two apart."
 walls_postfix_half: "A mark after walls reads after a scene or a concept ((bread)! and <bread>! read whole in the line reader) and not after a mode or a body ([bread]!, {bread}! and oven{bread}! leave the ! unread). The spoken reader names the mark in all five, and the parser makes it a second statement in all five."
 labels_need_newer: "A pair label is read only by the spoken reader and scanPairLabels (release 2026.11.13). The line reader of today's seed and of the canvas's bundle draws _load as a part of the body and leaves the closing _load unread. With semicolons inside a labeled body the spoken reader says wash;, dry; and names no sequence."
 cost_shape: "Parse cost follows the count of parts, not characters: 0.1 to 0.2 ms for each bare word, list item or nested level, alike on all three readers. A quoted sentence or a dated stream entry is one token however long. Quoting the sentence in the revision file's scene takes that line from 2.87 ms to 0.82 ms. A register stamp, [reg=set] or [reg=facet], costs 0.5 to 0.8 ms each."
 cost_across_pins: "One warm pass over the 26 files, under default parse options in node, costs 160 ms on the current seed and 160 ms on spw.quest's pinned parser. By this measure the cost the site consumer reported did not move between 27fa1e3d and cf41ca22."
 ocean_dialect: "The four ocean files that fail the probe (query/dialect, hot, q, sel) degrade to prose only because their path holds /query/, which selects the Spw.q dialect. With no path, or with the folder renamed in the path, each parses clean."
 prior_art_gap: "spw.quest's examples, starters, forms and bench hold no pair label, no mark after a bare name, one mark after walls (the headings file, a bond), no quantity sheet and no camelCase name."
}

^"audit"{
 rule: "Counting rule: names are IDENTIFIER tokens of seed's lexer plus one for each particle; marks are its OPERATOR, CONNECTOR and PARTICLE tokens. A prefix mark is a run of ! ^ ~ ? * = @ & $ % . glued in front of a name, binding heads such as @chip: included. A list is a comma, and a sequence a semicolon, written directly inside a body and outside quotes."
 timing: "Timed on the workbench's current seed, not on spw.quest's pinned reader. The pinned reader and the canvas's bundle were timed beside it and agree with it to within about two milliseconds a file. us/c is microseconds per character; the median file costs 15."
 outliers: "Dearest per character: revision 44 us/c (43 bare words inside braces), record 27 and vocabulary 23 (sets of backticked items with register stamps), perspectives 23 (four slots on each of four lines). Cheapest: notebook 1.8, since each dated entry is one token."
 never_shown: "Across the 26 files: 0 labels, 0 marks after a bare name, 1 mark after walls. 6 files write a sequence and 16 a list. The first line carries pivots in every file, 3 on an anchor and 4 on #[episode]{, and none of them is a line a reader would want."
 notebook: "Bench. 460 chars, 11 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 0.83 ms, 1.8 us/c. Names 6, marks 9. First line #>lab_notebook, 3 pivots. Shows 2 prefix marks. Canvas holds a line of 362 chars with 3 pivots, chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 record: "Bench. 330 chars, 12 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 9.04 ms, 27.4 us/c (outlier). Names 16, marks 19. First line #>component_chip, 3 pivots. Shows 3 prefix marks. Canvas holds a line of 204 chars with 4 pivots, chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 vocabulary: "Bench. 376 chars, 14 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 8.62 ms, 22.9 us/c (outlier). Names 17, marks 17. First line #>theme_vocabulary, 3 pivots. Shows none of the five; no prefix mark. Canvas holds a line of 159 chars with 4 pivots, chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 question: "Bench. 420 chars, 9 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 3.4 ms, 8.1 us/c. Names 7, marks 9. First line #>wonder_ring_on_glass, 3 pivots. Shows 3 prefix marks. Canvas holds a line of 337 chars with 18 pivots, chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 script: "Bench. 158 chars, 6 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 1.72 ms, 10.9 us/c. Names 15, marks 14. First line #>catalog_session, 3 pivots. Shows 5 prefix marks, a sequence. Canvas holds a binding of 52 chars with 0 pivots as held (12 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 revision: "Bench. 242 chars, 5 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 10.7 ms, 44.2 us/c (outlier). Names 43, marks 4. First line #[episode]{, 4 pivots. Shows none of the five; no prefix mark. Canvas holds a line of 230 chars with 17 pivots, chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 persona: "Bench. 292 chars, 9 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 3.13 ms, 10.7 us/c. Names 9, marks 9. First line #>archivist_persona, 3 pivots. Shows none of the five; no prefix mark. Canvas holds a line of 244 chars with 4 pivots, chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 calibration: "Study. 306 chars, 15 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 3.98 ms, 13 us/c. Names 15, marks 14. First line #>calibration, 3 pivots. Shows 1 prefix mark. Canvas holds a line of 28 chars with 3 pivots, chosen as the line that joins the others."
 perspectives: "Study. 262 chars, 10 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 5.99 ms, 22.9 us/c (outlier). Names 33, marks 14. First line #>four_readings, 3 pivots. Shows 2 prefix marks, a list. Canvas holds a line of 32 chars with 3 pivots, chosen as its & line."
 loops: "Board. 585 chars, 19 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 7.54 ms, 12.9 us/c. Names 34, marks 33. First line #>persona_loops, 3 pivots. Shows 9 prefix marks, a list, a sequence. Canvas holds a binding of 84 chars with 0 pivots as held (24 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 gradient: "Board. 465 chars, 17 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 5.81 ms, 12.5 us/c. Names 16, marks 14. First line #>gradient_space, 3 pivots. Shows 1 prefix mark. Canvas holds a binding of 176 chars with 0 pivots as held (4 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 syntax: "Stage. 284 chars, 10 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 4.26 ms, 15 us/c. Names 32, marks 18. First line #>syntax_stage, 3 pivots. Shows 4 prefix marks, a list, a sequence. Canvas holds a binding of 37 chars with 0 pivots as held (0 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 headings: "Stage. 364 chars, 11 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 2.7 ms, 7.4 us/c. Names 14, marks 10. First line #>subject_headings, 3 pivots. Shows 1 prefix mark, 1 mark after walls (a bond), a list. Canvas holds a binding of 47 chars with 0 pivots as held (7 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 perform: "Stage. 369 chars, 13 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 3.04 ms, 8.2 us/c. Names 17, marks 12. First line #>pattern_performance, 3 pivots. Shows 2 prefix marks. Canvas holds a binding of 38 chars with 0 pivots as held (2 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 outline: "Trial. 229 chars, 13 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 4.35 ms, 19 us/c. Names 25, marks 6. First line #>course_outline, 3 pivots. Shows 1 prefix mark, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 135 chars with 0 pivots as held (5 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 recurrence: "Trial. 202 chars, 8 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 3.49 ms, 17.3 us/c. Names 24, marks 11. First line #>rain_ledger, 3 pivots. Shows 2 prefix marks, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 35 chars with 0 pivots as held (1 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 laws: "Trial. 169 chars, 10 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 2.62 ms, 15.5 us/c. Names 17, marks 10. First line #>kitchen_laws, 3 pivots. Shows 5 prefix marks, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 95 chars with 0 pivots as held (12 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 pantry: "Trial. 176 chars, 9 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 2.78 ms, 15.8 us/c. Names 22, marks 4. First line #>pantry_census, 3 pivots. Shows a list; no prefix mark. Canvas holds a binding of 128 chars with 0 pivots as held (1 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 pipeline: "Team. 585 chars, 25 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 11.43 ms, 19.5 us/c. Names 48, marks 36. First line #>order_pipeline, 3 pivots. Shows 14 prefix marks, a list, a sequence. Canvas holds a binding of 47 chars with 0 pivots as held (4 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own; 1 line cut by the comment rule."
 review: "Team. 628 chars, 27 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 12.21 ms, 19.4 us/c. Names 43, marks 30. First line #>review_voice, 3 pivots. Shows 5 prefix marks, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 27 chars with 0 pivots as held (0 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 records: "Team. 617 chars, 19 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 9.39 ms, 15.2 us/c. Names 55, marks 21. First line #>engineering_records, 3 pivots. Shows 1 prefix mark, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 40 chars with 0 pivots as held (0 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 surfaces: "Team. 644 chars, 27 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 10.31 ms, 16 us/c. Names 71, marks 15. First line #>publishing_surfaces, 3 pivots. Shows 1 prefix mark, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 118 chars with 0 pivots as held (0 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 primitives: "Team. 624 chars, 18 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 9.27 ms, 14.8 us/c. Names 58, marks 16. First line #>primitives, 3 pivots. Shows 2 prefix marks, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 42 chars with 0 pivots as held (0 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
 algorithms: "Team. 722 chars, 21 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 10 ms, 13.8 us/c. Names 62, marks 47. First line #>algorithms, 3 pivots. Shows 19 prefix marks, a list, a sequence. Canvas holds a binding of 93 chars with 0 pivots as held (16 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own; 3 lines cut by the comment rule."
 role: "Team. 583 chars, 20 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 7.72 ms, 13.2 us/c. Names 32, marks 25. First line #>role_card, 3 pivots. Shows 6 prefix marks, a list. Canvas holds a binding of 47 chars with 0 pivots as held (2 on its value alone), chosen as the one its frame names."
 onboarding: "Team. 575 chars, 16 lines. 0 errors, 0 warnings. 5.89 ms, 10.2 us/c. Names 37, marks 33. First line #>team_onboarding, 3 pivots. Shows 14 prefix marks, a list, a sequence. Canvas holds a binding of 63 chars with 0 pivots as held (12 on its value alone), chosen as the first with parts of its own."
}

^"strangers_eye"{
 measure: "Names to marks by group: Bench 113 to 81, Study 48 to 28, Board 50 to 47, Stage 63 to 40, Trial 88 to 31, Team 406 to 223."
 opening_lines: "In spw.quest's editor every file opens with notation: a frame anchor, a tag line, then up to four @name: path lines. A stranger reads 2 to 6 such lines before the first line of the file's own matter. Only the revision file has none."
 bench: "Bench gives a stranger dates and sentences (the notebook, the question, the persona) and notation (the record and the vocabulary: #[ ] sets, [reg=set], [reg=facet]). Its names are the maker's shop words (chip, pill, ring, matte, glass), which name nothing a stranger already knows. Marks equal or outnumber names in 5 of 7 files."
 study: "Study gives a before and an after with two numbers (confidence 0.7, then 0.5) and four people looking at one chapter. These are names and a relationship. The canvas arrives on the two shortest lines of the shelf, %confidence{before ~> after} and &ch2{recall, spacing, kept, gap}."
 board: "Board gives three roles at one scan (archivist, designer, editor) and what each minds, which reads as people. The gradient file lands on four sentences with numbers. Marks nearly equal names because every loop and question wears one."
 stage: "Stage is built to show notation and does: six forms of one line, then pattern = pivot and performs = one.slot. Its names are placeholders (student, classmate, home, topics), so a stranger takes the forms and nothing to care about."
 trial: "Trial is the row a stranger can read: rain, a kitchen (bread, soup, spice, stock), a pantry of sixteen foods, a course outline. Names lead marks 88 to 31 and every arrival line is names first. These four are also the only files that ask the canvas something."
 team: "Team gives names a software team knows (orders, checkout, latency, binary_search). On the canvas 7 of 8 arrive on their first binding and not on their subject, and two are cut by the comment rule."
 lead_with_notation: "On the canvas's first screen these lead with notation: record, vocabulary and persona (a quoted block name, name = phrase fields, a register stamp), gradient (a #[ ] set of four sentences), notebook (four dated entries, 362 characters as a heading) and algorithms after the cut. These lead with names: laws, pantry, recurrence, outline, perspectives, loops, surfaces."
 zero_context: "The site consumer's two zero-context readers read names, prices, domains and the palette, and no sigil. None of the 26 files carries a price or a domain. The numbers a stranger meets are dates, two confidences, four gradient positions and a few measures inside sentences."
}

^"proposed"{
 shape: "Ten scripts, 32 to 158 characters, 2 to 6 lines. Each avoids what the audit found costly or lost: no # after a space, no tag line, no path line, no register stamp, no bare sentence in braces. Each reads complete with 0 errors and 0 warnings on the current seed, on spw.quest's pinned parser and on the canvas's bundle. Every line went through probe.mts; outputs are in examples-scratch/proposed-probe.txt and proposed-harness.txt."
 coverage: "Prefix against walls-postfix: two_sides. A list and a sequence: both_ways. A labeled loop: two_loops. Many pivots at first touch: first_touch (11) and soup_phases (23). A camelCase cluster scene: contact_card. A quantity sheet with a dimensionless group: walk_or_run. The ocean's distance: from_the_kitchen. Its tiers: within_reach. Its phases: soup_phases. A first screen read without the language: corner_cafe."

 two_sides_pitch: "The same mark on either side of the same name: set the alarm, then the alarm goes off."
 two_sides_script: `#>two_sides
!(alarm)
(alarm)!
?(forecast)
(forecast)?`
 two_sides_asks: "Hold line one and step to line two. Draw a mark ahead of the walls and a mark behind them differently, and offer the other side as one touch (the side pivot)."
 two_sides_screenshot: "Today: the heading !(alarm) and one node ! with a scene alarm, and the same picture again for line two. With sides drawn: alarm with the act ahead of it, then behind it."
 two_sides_reader: "Parses on all three readers. The canvas's bundled reader gives both lines the same slots, so the difference needs the fragment and pivot readers (339728cf, after release 2026.11.13). The two spoken readings need spokenLine (2026.11.13)."
 two_sides_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 1.13 ms for 53 characters. Said: action scene alarm; scene alarm, action; wonder scene forecast; scene forecast, wonder. 4 pivots on each line; the side pivot is read back by the line reader and the spoken reader, and the parser reads each postfix line as 2 statements. The canvas holds !(alarm) as the first statement."
 two_sides_prior: "Prior art: forms.js holds ?garden{seed} against garden{?seed}, a mark in front against a mark inside. No spw.quest line puts a mark after walls."

 both_ways_pitch: "Three things in a morning, once side by side and once in order."
 both_ways_script: `#>morning
morning{coffee, shower, email}
morning{coffee; shower; email}`
 both_ways_asks: "Hold line one, then step to line two. The canvas starts a node that is still there from where it was, so coffee, shower and email should hold near their places while the figure changes, a ring to a row."
 both_ways_screenshot: "morning as the heading word and three plain names in a ring: one container and nothing to decode but two braces."
 both_ways_reader: "Reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today. The said difference, list of 3 against sequence of 3, needs spokenLine (2026.11.13)."
 both_ways_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 1.29 ms for 71 characters. Line reader: separator comma and semicolon, 3 parts each. Said: morning, definition, list of 3: coffee, shower, email; and sequence of 3 for line two. The canvas holds line one as the first with parts of its own."
 both_ways_prior: "Prior art: forms.js and bench.js each hold a list and a sequence as single lines, each opened alone by ?spw=. Missing is one script that carries both, so the step between them is the lesson."

 two_loops_pitch: "Two ways to come back: a day that ends in day, and a load of laundry that says again."
 two_loops_script: `#>two_loops
day{wake{work{rest{day}}}}
laundry{_load wash dry fold ~again ^_load }_load`
 two_loops_asks: "Line one: draw the inner day as the outer day returning; by its source the canvas ties words that recur in one line with a faint echo. Line two: draw the label as one thing with two ends, and ^_load as the way back to its open."
 two_loops_screenshot: "By source, not seen: day at the top and four bodies nested inside it, the innermost holding day again, tied to the first by an echo."
 two_loops_reader: "Line one reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today. Line two needs scanPairLabels and spokenLine (2026.11.13); on the canvas's reader and on today's line reader _load is drawn as a part and the closing _load is left unread."
 two_loops_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 2.03 ms for 87 characters. Said: day, definition, wake, definition, work, definition, rest, definition, day; end work; end wake; end day. And: laundry, definition, labeled load, wash, dry, fold, potential again, ascension load; end load. The parser reads line two as 2 statements."
 two_loops_prior: "Prior art: the bench and the outline file each nest four deep; neither ends in its own name. spw.quest holds no pair label anywhere."

 first_touch_pitch: "One short line with eleven lines one touch away."
 first_touch_script: `#>first_touch
=rent@(<landlord>)`
 first_touch_asks: "Offer the pivots at first touch: turn the mark (*rent, @rent), move it to the other side (rent=), turn the bond (~ ^ &), turn the wall around landlord, exchange the two sides."
 first_touch_screenshot: "rent and landlord, two names and one bond between them: what is set, and by whose view."
 first_touch_reader: "Reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader as a bond @ between two sides. The eleven pivots need pivotsOf (339728cf), which is in no bundle of release 2026.11.13 and in no host."
 first_touch_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 0.55 ms for 32 characters. Said: configuration rent, perspective, scene concept landlord. 11 pivots, 10 read back by the line reader; =rent @(<landlord>) is the one it does not read. The parser reads the line as 2 statements."
 first_touch_prior: "Prior art: the owner's =foo@(<bar>), with names a stranger pays. Of the 26 files only the experiment's question (18) and the revision (17) arrive on a line with more pivots, and both are sentences."

 contact_card_pitch: "Four names that share their halves: home and work, phone and email."
 contact_card_script: `#>contact_card
contact = card{homePhone, workPhone, homeEmail, workEmail}
home{Phone, Email}
work{Phone, Email}
Phone{home, work}
Email{home, work}`
 contact_card_asks: "Spread the four parts and cluster them by hump. By the first hump they gather as home and work, by the last as Phone and Email, and each name sits in two clusters. Lines three to six are those clusters written out, for a canvas that has no hump tool."
 contact_card_screenshot: "contact = card and four camelCase names in a ring: a thing every phone already holds."
 contact_card_reader: "Every line reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today. The clustering is new: the canvas's cluster command groups by colour, its map by operator, lens, frame, shape or whole, and nothing in it splits a word at a capital."
 contact_card_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 2.38 ms for 147 characters. The canvas holds the contact line because the file is named for it. Said: contact, configuration card, definition, list of 4: homePhone, workPhone, homeEmail, workEmail. The spoken reader says each camelCase name unbroken."
 contact_card_prior: "Prior art: none; no spw.quest script holds a camelCase name. The recurrence file (rain) is its nearest kin: one word held still while its slot changes."

 walk_or_run_pitch: "Why a walk turns into a run: three measurements and the one number that has no unit."
 walk_or_run_script: `#>walk_or_run
^walk{
 %speed[1.4 m/s]
 %leg[0.9 m]
 %g[9.81 m/s^2]
}`
 walk_or_run_asks: "Draw three measures, each with its unit as a lens, and beside them the one group that survives any change of units: speed squared over leg times g."
 walk_or_run_screenshot: "walk, and under it speed 1.4 m/s, leg 0.9 m, g 9.81 m/s^2: numbers with units a stranger knows from school."
 walk_or_run_reader: "The canvas's bundled reader reads it whole as a sequence of three, since its splitter joins the lines of a body with semicolons. The group needs the quantity sheet reader (release 2026.11.13, spw sheet --pi), which the canvas does not bundle."
 walk_or_run_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 4.59 ms for 68 characters, the dearest of the ten at 67 us/c because each unit is several parts. spw sheet --pi gives one group: speed^2 times leg^-1 times g^-1 = 0.2219957. The sheet reads the same with semicolons between its terms. The spoken reader says the power in m/s^2 as ascension, 2."
 walk_or_run_prior: "Prior art: the pendulum sheet in the workbench's dimensional-analysis theory. spw.quest holds no sheet."

 from_the_kitchen_pitch: "How much of the town you can see from the kitchen: the room whole, the house at the rim, the street as a count, the town as fog."
 from_the_kitchen_script: `#>from_the_kitchen
kitchen[whole]{stove, sink, table}
house[rim]{kitchen, hall, bedroom}
street[count]{house, bakery, school}
town[fog]{street, river, market}`
 from_the_kitchen_asks: "Hold kitchen and place the other three by their distance from it. Hold street instead and the four words in brackets should trade places. Each mode says the detail one distance function would compute, so the script is its own expected answer."
 from_the_kitchen_screenshot: "kitchen with whole framed above it, and stove, sink, table in a ring. A stranger reads a room."
 from_the_kitchen_reader: "Every line reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today. Rim and fog need the distance function and the disk kernel the workbench's journal proposes for the ocean; no code computes them yet."
 from_the_kitchen_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 4.52 ms for 158 characters. Said: kitchen, mode whole, definition, list of 3: stove, sink, table. The canvas holds the kitchen line as the first with parts of its own."
 from_the_kitchen_prior: "Prior art: the outline and bench nesting files hold containment in one line. None writes the same containment as separate lines a reader can stand inside."

 within_reach_pitch: "Three shelves by how long a thing waits untouched: the counter for 8 beats, the fridge for 64, the freezer for 256."
 within_reach_script: `#>within_reach
counter[8]{bread, butter, tea}
fridge[64]{milk, eggs, soup}
freezer[256]{peas, stock, ice}
soup{freezer ~> fridge ~> counter}`
 within_reach_asks: "Draw the three bodies nearer or farther by their number, let a part fade as its beats run out, and move soup one shelf closer when a hand touches it. The last line is that path."
 within_reach_screenshot: "counter with 8 framed above it, and bread, butter, tea: a kitchen and a number to wonder about."
 within_reach_reader: "Every line reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today. The fading and the promotion need the ocean's tier contract (hot 8, warm 64, cold 256, promote on access, demote after the beats pass) offered to a host as something to draw."
 within_reach_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 2.74 ms for 140 characters. Said: counter, mode 8, definition, list of 3: bread, butter, tea; and soup, definition, movement of 3: freezer, fridge, counter."
 within_reach_prior: "Prior art: the bench reads numbers as words (2026[season]{10, 11, 12}). No spw.quest file gives a number a job."

 soup_phases_pitch: "Six steps of a soup, each wearing the mark and the wall of one ocean phase."
 soup_phases_script: `#>soup_phases
soup{?~taste; <broth>; @(stove); &[season]; *{bowl}; ^table}`
 soup_phases_asks: "Draw the body as a row of six and let each step show its phase: taste as a probe, broth as a name, stove as a place to watch from, season as a gathering, bowl as the thing made, table as the lift."
 soup_phases_screenshot: "soup, and a row of six kitchen words in order, each in a different wall: taste, broth, stove, season, bowl, table."
 soup_phases_reader: "Reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today, 6 parts in order. The phase names come from the ocean's ph/p1 to p6, and no host reads them yet."
 soup_phases_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 2.3 ms for 74 characters; 23 pivots. Said: soup, definition, sequence of 6: wonder potential, taste, concept broth, perspective scene stove, subject mode season, value definition, bowl; ascension table; end soup."
 soup_phases_prior: "Prior art: the session and pipeline files write a path whose steps wear marks. Missing is the ocean's own order, a mark with a wall: ?~, < >, @( ), &[ ], *{ }, ^."

 corner_cafe_pitch: "A menu: three drinks and their prices."
 corner_cafe_script: `#>corner_cafe
cafe[morning]{espresso[3.50], latte[4.50], cortado[4.00]}<menu>`
 corner_cafe_asks: "Draw each price as the lens on its drink, and keep all three readable on a phone."
 corner_cafe_screenshot: "cafe, morning, and espresso 3.50, latte 4.50, cortado 4.00: names and prices, the two things the site consumer's zero-context readers read without help."
 corner_cafe_reader: "Reads whole on the canvas's bundled reader today, with each price as its drink's mode."
 corner_cafe_probe: "0 errors, 0 warnings; 3.11 ms for 77 characters. Said: cafe, mode morning, definition, list of 3: espresso, mode 3.50, latte, mode 4.50, cortado, mode 4.00; end cafe, concept menu."
 corner_cafe_prior: "Prior art: the five starters are four-slot lines of working nouns (launch, funnel, page, role, search), and the bench has a part with rooms of its own. Missing is a first screen a stranger reads before learning anything."
}

^"ocean_health"{
 summary: "probe.mts file over all 35 surfaces: 0 errors anywhere; 31 parse with 0 warnings; 4 read as prose with 1 warning each. A surface costs 10.8 to 62.1 ms for 1,253 to 4,355 characters."
 table_root: "atlas, index, libs, lit, lsp, phase, runtime, shape, style, trace, ux: each 0 errors, 0 warnings."
 table_algos: "algos/cache, algos/cluster, algos/geom, algos/material: each 0 errors, 0 warnings."
 table_ph: "ph/p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6: each 0 errors, 0 warnings."
 table_expr: "expr/arc, expr/custom, expr/rel, expr/util, links/links, derivatives/derivs: each 0 errors, 0 warnings."
 table_rest: "experiments/syntax, spells/spells (the dearest at 62.1 ms), template/t, ops/cycle: each 0 errors, 0 warnings."
 table_query: "query/dialect, query/hot, query/q, query/sel: each 0 errors, 1 warning (prose-degradation), read as prose."
 query_cause: "The four are not broken text. parse(text, { path }) infers the Spw.q dialect from /query/ in the path, and under it the surface consumes nothing. The same text with no path, or with the path's folder renamed to expr, parses clean with 0 warnings."
 stale_sequence: "The spirit sequence the atlas, the phase registry and the syntax experiments quote, ?~<#.>@(#.)&[#.]*{#.}^, is read by the parser as one statement, but the line reader stops before *{#.}^ and the pivot reader offers 21 pivots of which none reads back. Each phase token alone reads whole."
 stale_postfix: "experiments/syntax keeps a postfix bank of data~ and x!. Today the spoken reader says each unbroken as part of the name and the parser splits each into 2 statements, which is the seed's trailing row. Its form_b, with bars between forms, leaves 9 characters unread."
 stale_paths: "Of 41 root-relative paths the ocean cites, 4 do not exist: .spw/gen/lens/core.spw, probe.spw, relation.spw and .spw/gen/build/tide-lift.spw, all cited by spells and the query layer as projections."
 stale_vintage: "The ocean is written in the syntax of last summer: 85 bare-colon bodies (key: { }), 158 bare arrays, 135 lines with an inline // comment, against 2 facets written .{ }, 2 #[ ] sets and no register stamp. All 35 files end in generated wonders, 72 blocks in all, that ask about the file's own reading (its metabolic signature, its absent concept) and name no host."
 stale_words: "The seed cites a workbench plan note that counts five things called ocean, one of them an unimplemented cluster algorithm. The cache algorithm's channel list names ocean beside experimental, and cluster.spw states its distance as weighted overlap on keys plus ref intersection."
 near_examples: "Nearest to examples as they stand, each reading whole on the canvas's bundled reader: atlas plant_cycles (kelp, seagrass, mangrove; 198 characters), atlas electrolytes (five salts, each tied to a mark; 306), algos/cache tiers (hot, warm, cold with 8, 64, 256; 754) and a material property block such as viscosity (250). Each would arrive as one long heading of sentences, so a stranger's version pulls the nouns out of the quoted prose."
 near_phases: "ph/p1 to p6 each carry a four-line physics list (liminality, frequency, coupling, cache tier). Read across the six files they are one table of six rows, which is the content of soup_phases."
 near_geom: "algos/geom writes five formulas as nested arrays. The quantity sheet reader now computes the same thing in one line: ^graph{ %nodes[12] %edges[30] %rho[@edges/(@nodes*(@nodes-1))] } gives rho 0.2272727 with dimension 1."
 wonder_distance: "Would a refreshed ocean hold one distance a host can ask for from any held line, answering whole, rim, count or fog, so the canvas, spw.quest's shelf and an editor's folds agree on what is near?"
 wonder_tiers: "Would the tiers of 8, 64 and 256 beats be something a host draws, a part fading and a touch moving it closer, and not only a cache's bookkeeping?"
 wonder_phases: "Would the six phases be an order a host turns through, each a mark with a wall, the way pivotsOf already turns a mark along an order the host supplies?"
 wonder_names: "Would the ocean's own figures be its examples: six biomes with one role each, five salts with one mark each, three plants with one loop each?"
 wonder_dialect: "Would a host that mounts the ocean want the path to choose the dialect at all, when it turns four clean files into prose?"
}

^"path"{
 link: "On spw.quest each file's Canvas link is factshiftScriptHref(example.script): https://factshift.center/#spw64= followed by the script as unpadded base64url. The fragment never reaches a server. The five starters use the same link for one line. The bench and the answer card use the other door, ?spw= with one line of up to 400 characters."
 first_file: "The file a stranger meets first is EXAMPLES[0], the lab notebook: the guide opens it by default, with its Canvas link in the file's head. Above it on the page sit the five starters, each with a one-line Canvas link of its own."
 loads: "The canvas reads the fragment once and removes it, splits the text into top-level statements, adds each as a row of its thread (40 rows at most), and holds one of them: the arrival line."
 arrival_rules: "In order: a line that starts with & and has parts; a binding named by a word of the #>frame; a line whose body names two or more of the file's named statements and is not named itself; the first line with parts of its own; else the first statement."
 held: "Holding a line sets the page title and the heading to the line as written, rewrites the address to ?spw= with that one line, and draws the line's anatomy in a column at most 620 px wide from 8 percent down the stage."
 address_after: "After landing, the address carries the held line and not the script. A stranger who copies the address shares one line; the rest of the file stays in that browser's thread."
 three_seconds: "From source, in the first three seconds a stranger sees: the held line as a heading in raw Spw; its word and parts as nodes; a note near the handle that says held and the rule that chose the line, which the site consumer's motion cache measured holding 2.4 s; the thread opened as lines and a count; a status line with the count and the words from a link."
 note_words: "For 17 of 26 files that note reads: held, the first with parts of its own. It speaks of the file's structure and not of its matter, in the register the owner called verbose."
 thread_top: "The thread lists rows newest first, and a loaded file's rows are added in reverse, so the file's own order shows top down. Its top rows are the placing rows: the frame, the first tag, and each @name: path line, 2 to 6 rows of sigils above the first row with a name in it. Stepping skips them, but they are drawn."
 change_one: "Smallest change one: in the pipeline and algorithms files, never write a # mark after a space. Glued to its separator (;#seen{node}, ;#keep{answer}, ;#index{customer}) each line still parses with 0 errors and survives the canvas's comment rule: algorithms arrives as 11 rows and not 7, and the pipeline keeps all 11 steps."
 change_two: "Smallest change two: let each file say where to land, by a frame word that names the binding or by one & line. Renaming only the pipeline file's frame, #>order_pipeline to #>order_steps, makes the canvas hold the eleven steps, as the file is named for it, where it now holds the source line. The #steps anchors that review, algorithms and role point to stay, and order_pipeline is written nowhere else in spw.quest. Review, records, surfaces, primitives, algorithms and onboarding fall to their first binding the same way."
 change_three: "Smallest change three: lead the shelf with a file whose arrival is names. The lab notebook arrives as a 362-character heading of four dated entries, 6 names to 9 marks. The kitchen file arrives as 95 characters holding bread, soup, spice and stock; each of the ten proposed scripts arrives in 8 to 63 characters. spw.quest's route test holds the tab order to the shelf's order and expects #>lab_notebook painted first, so one test line moves with it."
 not_a_change: "Outside this lens, since it changes an app and not an example: the comment rule itself, and whether the Canvas link carries a file's path lines at all, since they resolve only on spw.quest's shelf."
}

^"broken"{
 claim: "separators_clock: commas are things side by side (lanes), semicolons one after another (a strip), and a plain space might be overlap, two things in one place like a chord."
 holds: "The semicolon half holds. The canvas draws every separator but the comma as ordinal, a row, and the spoken reader says sequence of 3."
 comma: "The comma is weaker than stated. The canvas draws a comma body as a ring, in common, and spw.quest's forms say of it: both are there at once, and their order says nothing. The comma already means what the claim gives to the space. It is the chord, not lanes."
 space: "The space is contradicted. morning{coffee shower email} is 1 part on the seed's line reader and on the canvas's bundled reader, so it is drawn as one node of three words, not two things in one place. The spoken reader says the three words and names no join."
 taken: "The space already has three jobs in the text at hand: a sentence inside a body (the revision file's scenes), multiplication in a quantity sheet (a space between atoms multiplies), and the gap between a sheet's terms. And the canvas reads a line break inside braces as a semicolon, so a new line is sequence too."
 also_weak: "label_particles says labels are the only construct that crosses the tree. The canvas's source already ties any two nodes of one name with an echo and no label, which is how day{wake{work{rest{day}}}} would draw as a loop today; and 20 of 26 files point across files by a path, some to a named line. Only is wrong."
}

^"wonders"{
 two_shelves: "Are files people keep and lines that carry a stranger the same shelf? The 26 are the first kind; the ten here are the second, and the starters sit between."
 says_where_to_land: "Would a file say its own arrival in Spw, an & line or a frame word, so spw.quest, the canvas and an editor land on the same line by one rule a reader can see?"
 hash_after_space: "Which wins after a space, a # that starts a comment or a # that marks a name? The parser reads #seen{node} as a mark; the canvas's splitter reads it as a comment."
 bindings_pivot: "Would a pivot reader that reads name = value turn 17 silent arrival lines into the richest ones on the shelf?"
 anchor_is_not_a_fragment: "Should an anchor or a tag line offer any pivot at all, when each one offered breaks it?"
 mark_after_any_wall: "Is a mark after a body, oven{bread}!, meant to read like a mark after a scene, (bread)!? Today two walls take a postfix and two do not."
 side_on_the_canvas: "What would the canvas draw for a mark behind the walls: the same node on the other edge, a second beat, a residue?"
 price_first: "Would a first screen with a price on it be the cheapest marketing the shelf can do, given what zero-context readers read?"
 asks_everywhere: "Only the Trial row says what it asks of the canvas. Would every file that opens there say it?"
 expected_in_the_script: "from_the_kitchen writes its expected answer in its own brackets. Would other ocean examples carry what a host should draw the same way, so an example is also a test?"
 ocean_as_kitchen: "The three ocean examples all found a kitchen. Is that a sign the ocean's metaphors (reef, tide, salt) are a second vocabulary a stranger must learn before the first?"
}

^"unverified"{
 no_browser: "I opened no browser and fetched no live site. Everything about what the canvas draws is read from its source and from spw.quest's bench descriptions, not seen."
 deployed: "That the comment rule and the arrival rule are deployed rests on the built asset in the site consumer's tree (index-IXaaHWiT.js, built 2026-11-14 from app 1a4157a), which contains both. The live site was not checked."
 load_time: "No time-to-first-paint for factshift.center with a carried script is measured anywhere I read. The motion cache's settle times of 7.6 to 18.8 s are for the site consumer's own pages; for the canvas it measured clocks only, on a local build of a branch that is not deployed."
 phone_layout: "Whether the opened thread covers the held line's nodes on a phone in those first seconds is not known."
 strangers: "No stranger read any of this. What a first screen says is my reading, leaning on two zero-context model readers who saw other screenshots, not these examples."
 ocean_reading: "The seed's reading of planned improvements to the ocean is not confirmed by the owner, and the three ocean examples lean on it."
 walk_to_run: "That people break into a run near a group value of 0.5 is from recall, with no source read. The script's own arithmetic (0.222 at 1.4 m/s) is computed."
 quest_slot_reader: "The proposals were read by spw.quest's pinned parser, not by its own slot reader (specimen-reading.js), which its bench says stops at a nested brace. Its tests were not run."
 worker_cost: "Timings are warm, in node, on one machine. The site consumer's 41 ms and 2 ms for the revision and notebook files were measured on its own pin and setup; here they are 10.7 ms and 0.83 ms, the same order and a smaller ratio."
 hump_names: "Whether a stranger reads homePhone as home and Phone is assumed."
}

